BCC_Q({"y": 2016, "n": 11, "part": "C", "topic": "pat", "title": "Replacing Shapes", "answer": 1, "story": "<p>Alice the beaver plays a game with shaped cards. She starts with a\nsingle card that is a square and uses the following set of replacement\nrules.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><img alt=\"S to T,T\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:35px\"/>\nmeaning each square card is replaced by two triangle cards, and<\/li>\n<li><img alt=\"T to S,T,S\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:35px\"/>\nmeaning each triangle card is replaced by one square card, one triangle and another square (in that order).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The result of following these rules three times is shown below.<\/p>\n<div class=\"center\">\n<p><img alt=\"S to T,T to S,T,S,S,T,S to T,T,S,T,S,T,T,T,T,S,T,S,T,T\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:35px\"/><\/p>\n<\/div>\n<p>Boris the beaver plays a similar game. He starts with one of a\nsquare, triangle or circle and he plays with a different set of\nreplacement rules. The result of following these rules three times is\nshown below.<\/p>\n<div class=\"center\">\n<p><img alt=\"T,S,S,C,C,S,C,C,S,T,S,T, where C represents a Circle.\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:35px\"/><\/p>\n<\/div>", "question": "<p>Which of the following set of replacement rules might Boris have\nused?<\/p>\n<ol class=\"mc\">\n<p><li><img alt=\"S to T,S,S, and T to C, and C to T,T\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:170px\"/> <\/li><\/p>\n<p><li><img alt=\"S to S,C,C, and T to T,S, and C to S,T\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:170px\"/> <\/li><\/p>\n<p><li><img alt=\"T to T,T, and S to C,C, and C to T,S,T\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:170px\"/> <\/li><\/p>\n<p><li><img alt=\"T to S,C, and S to T,T,T, and C to S,T\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:170px\"/><\/li><\/p>\n<\/ol>", "answerHtml": "<p>(B)\n<img alt=\"S to S,C,C, and T to T,S, and C to S,T\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:170px\"/> <\/p>", "explanation": "<p>Notice that<\/p>\n<div class=\"center\">\n<p><img alt=\"T to T,S to T,S,S,C,C to T,S,S,C,C,S,C,C,S,T,S,T\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:35px\"/><\/p>\n<\/div>\n<p>so Boris might have used the rules in (B). To be complete, we should\nrule out the other possibilities.<\/p>\n<p>For answer (A), if Boris starts with a triangle or circle, he will\nnever generate any square cards. So using these rules three times\nstarting with a square, the result is<\/p>\n<div class=\"center\">\n<p><img alt=\"S to T,S,S to C,T,S,S,T,S,S to T,T,C,T,S,S,T,S,S,C,T,S,S,T,S,S\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:30px\"/><\/p>\n<\/div>\n<p>and the line of cards can only get longer using the rules more often.\nAlternatively, we can notice that he can never generate two circles\nbeside each other.<\/p>\n<p>For answer (C), we notice that Boris can never generate two squares\nbeside each other.<\/p>\n<p>For answer (D), we notice that Boris can never generate two circles\nbeside each other.<\/p>", "connections": "<p>The rules presented here describe a sequence of rewriting rules given\nby a <em>context-free grammar<\/em>. These grammar systems can\ndescribe<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>natural phenomena, such as the growth of plants,<\/li>\n<li>natural language, such as how to form sentences, and<\/li>\n<li>formal languages, such as how programming languages are\nstructured.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The question here is asking for a <em>derivation<\/em> or a\n<em>parse<\/em> of a given <em>word<\/em> using different rules. Parsing\nis one important step in translating a program from “human-readable”\nwords to “computer-readable” binary numbers.<\/p>", "mc": 1});